+# =============================================================================
+# S3 — THE HIERARCHY WALK, THE SAVE-CARD BLOCK, AND THE .save FILE WRITER
+# =============================================================================
+# doc/claude/specs/op_annotation.md §3 (R2/R4), §5 (I1/I2/I4/I6), §6.
+#
+#   op_annot::save_cards {}       -> the `.save` block as TEXT, {} when empty
+#   op_annot::last_warnings {}    -> the warning LIST from the most recent walk
+#   op_annot::write_save_file {}  -> $netlist_dir/<cell>.save, returns the path
+#
+# Ported from sg13g2_sch_expand / sg13g2_hier_sch_expand (sg13g2_procs.tcl:345-421,
+# byte-identical twin at sky130A/sky130_procs.tcl:93-170) and de-PDK'd. What was
+# taken verbatim: the entry/exit flag block, the recursion body, the `nolist_libs`
+# skip, and the vector-instance handling (expandlabel for the multiplicity,
+# descend once at n==1 then `change_sch_path $n`, `go_back 2` at n==ninst) — the
+# piece src/xschem.tcl's generic `hier_traversal` lacks and the reason to port the
+# PDK version rather than the in-tree one. What was DROPPED: the per-PDK
+# `savelist` accumulator array, and `only_subckts` / `all_hierarchy` / `pattern`
+# (the descriptor's own `match` globs are the only cell filter — a regexp over the
+# cell name would be a competing second one). What MOVED: the prototypes'
+# `startpath` arithmetic, which is the right arithmetic in the wrong house — it
+# now lives inside the ONE name builder as the `deck` basis (op_annot::_pathfor),
+# so this file does no path arithmetic of its own (I1).
+#
+# ============================================================================
+# ⚠ THE CARD IS BARE. THIS FILE NEVER CALLS op_annot::vector.  (spec §3 R4)
+# ============================================================================
+# Re-measured on BOTH ngspice binaries on this box (/usr/bin = 42,
+# /usr/local/bin = 46+, which SHADOWS 42 on PATH):
+#
+#     .save @m.xm1.m1[id]     -> raw contains  i(@m.xm1.m1[id])
+#     .save i(@m.xm1.m1[id])  -> raw contains  NOTHING. no vector, no message.
+#
+# ngspice applies the wrapper itself from the parameter's own type. The emitter
+# writes `[op_annot::devpath $inst deck $root][param]`; `vector` is the READ
+# shape only.
+#
+# ============================================================================
+# ⚠ THE CARD IS **DECK**-BASED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF ISSUE 0436
+# ============================================================================
+# A save card goes into a deck NOBODY HAS SIMULATED YET, so which raw happens to
+# be open in the editor may not be an input to it. Attempt 1 of this step built
+# its cards from `devpath`'s default (`read`) basis, whose prefix comes from
+# `sim_sch_path` — measured FROM THE LEVEL A RAW WAS LOADED AT, and re-measured
+# at every step as the walk descends (draw.c:2825 `sch_waves_loaded()`
+# re-matching `raw->schname`, which save.c:1260 bound to whatever cell the user
+# stood in). Reproduced: with a raw open one level down, TWO distinct instances
+# of one cell produced ONE identical card, with no warning anywhere.
+#
+# So every card here is built with `deck` and the walk's ENTRY sch_path as its
+# root, i.e. the block is rooted at the cell the walk STARTED in.
+#
+# ⚠ WHY ENTRY-RELATIVE AND NOT LEVEL-0-ABSOLUTE (decision D2, and issue 0436's
+# own fix sketch says the other one). Measured tie-breaker: `xschem netlist`
+# invoked while descended into a cell writes THAT cell as the deck top
+# (`**.subckt <cell>`). So the deck a user simulates from level N is rooted at
+# level N and only an entry-relative card names a device that deck contains. It
+# is also what makes write_save_file's `<current cell>.save` filename agree with
+# its own body. The two definitions COINCIDE when the walk starts at currsch 0,
+# which is exactly why attempt 1's goldens could not see the difference.
+#
+# ============================================================================
+# ⚠ I2's SPELLING IS `.save all` (DOT-CARD), NOT the bare `save all`
+# ============================================================================
+# Spec §5 I2 and this step's brief both say "prepend `save all`". Taken
+# literally that is WORSE than emitting nothing. Measured on ngspice-42 AND
+# 46+: a bare deck-level `save all` line is `Error on line N … Unable to find
+# definition of model` — ngspice parses it as an `s`-prefixed switch instance —
+# and NO RAW FILE IS WRITTEN AT ALL, so it removes the whole raw rather than
+# only the node voltages R2 is about. The dot-card works on both. Both
+# consumers want a deck-level dot-card anyway: the `.include`-able <cell>.save
+# file, and ase.tcl:3161's render_deck (which already emits its own `.save all`
+# — a duplicate is harmless, measured).
+#
+# I2 is honoured as "any NON-EMPTY block carries `.save all` as its first
+# line". An empty walk returns {}, not a lone `.save all`: a file whose entire
+# content is `.save all` says nothing while reporting success, and {} is what
+# lets the caller name WHICH first-run confusion it hit (spec §4.6).
+#
+# ============================================================================
+# ⚠ THE WALK EMITS ONLY WHAT THE NETLISTER WOULD (issue 0437)
+# ============================================================================
+# A card for a device the deck does not contain is NOT harmless and does NOT
+# blank: ngspice writes a full column under exactly the name asked for, holding
+# 0.0, with only `Warning: unrecognized variable` on stderr (spec landmine 9).
+# That is I3's fabricated number arriving through the save file. So every
+# instance is put to the netlister's own filter (op_annot::_netlisted), which
+# mirrors skip_instance() (netlist.c:1245) — inst.flags | sym.flags — in Tcl:
+#
+#   * spice_ignore on the INSTANCE **or** on the SYMBOL, in util.c:72's truthy
+#     spellings plus exact-case `open` and `short` (actions.c:1055 / :960);
+#   * only_toplevel (instance attribute, truthy only) BELOW the walk entry —
+#     spice_netlist.c:216 gates it on `netlist_count`, i.e. on the cell being a
+#     subcircuit of the deck rather than its top, which is the same relativity
+#     decision D2 gives the path basis;
+#   * lvs_ignore on instance or symbol, gated on the user's `::lvs_ignore`
+#     global exactly as spice_netlist.c:209 passes it to skip_instance().
+#
+# ⚠ NOT `xschem translate <inst> @spice_ignore`, WHICH ISSUE 0437 PRESCRIBES.
+# Measured on this tree, that probe is wrong in BOTH directions: translate's
+# fallback is `sym->templ` only (token.c:5350-5353) and never reads
+# `sym->prop_ptr`, so it answers {} for a symbol-G-record `spice_ignore` (the
+# very case 0437 was filed about — the card would still be emitted) and `true`
+# for a template-borne one whose device IS in the netlist (a real device would
+# be dropped). The netlister-faithful pair is `getprop instance <n>
+# spice_ignore` OR `... cell::spice_ignore`.
+#
+# ⚠ AN IGNORED SUBCIRCUIT IS NOT DESCENDED INTO (decision D6), which is a
+# different edit in a different place from "emits no card". spice_netlist.c:464
+# writes a symbol's `.subckt` DEFINITION even when every calling instance is
+# ignored, but no call to it is written — so no device anywhere below that
+# instance exists in the run. A guard bolted onto the card emitter alone would
+# still walk in and name every one of them.
+#
+# ============================================================================
+# ⚠ NO PARAMETER VALIDATION. THE DESCRIPTOR IS THE ONLY POLICY ABOUT WHAT IS
+#    SAVABLE — WHICH IS WHY THE cgso/cgdo RULING IS A DESCRIPTOR EDIT (0429)
+# ============================================================================
+# save_cards does not filter PARAMETERS — not even ones a given ngspice
+# rejects. That is deliberate (I1): `params` is the single list the save side
+# and the display side share, and an emitter that dropped a parameter the
+# display still reads would be a second, silently drifting policy. The fix for
+# a parameter no simulator accepts belongs in the DESCRIPTOR data, and that is
+# where it was made: sky130's `cgso`/`cgdo` rows are gone
+# (sky130A/sky130_procs.tcl, issue 0429) because on ngspice-42 ONE such card
+# makes the run write no raw file at all under the `.control … write … .endc`
+# idiom every shipped PDK bench uses, exit status 0. REJECTED: a hardcoded
+# invalid-param blocklist inside this emitter, and validating against a real raw
+# at emit time (the raw does not exist yet — that is the whole point).
+#
+# ============================================================================
+# ⚠ I6 IS THE WHOLE RISK OF THIS FILE, AND IT IS FIVE THINGS
+# ============================================================================
+# The walk sets no_draw 1, no_undo 1, keep_symbols 1, pushes a log-suppress
+# scope and DESCENDS THE REAL DESIGN. Every exit path — including a raise three
+# levels down — restores all of them AND the entry hierarchy level. Before-state
+# on this tree: `sky130_save_fet_params` raises `Symbol not found` and returns
+# with no_draw=1 keep_symbols=1 still set (issue 0431). Shape:
+# `catch {_walk} res opts` -> an unconditional `_restore` whose every line is
+# individually catch-wrapped -> `return -options $opts $res`.
+#
+#  * THE UNWIND IS BOUNDED BY THE ENTRY LEVEL, not by 0. src/xschem.tcl:3857's
+#    `while {[xschem get currsch]} {xschem go_back}` would ascend past a caller
+#    that was already descended, leaving the user in a different cell than the
+#    one they invoked this from.
+#  * `xschem get no_undo` DOES NOT EXIST (setter only, scheduler.c:11958; it
+#    returns {} whether the flag is 0 or 1 — measured). So the entry value is
+#    unobservable and 0 is the only value that can be restored. Issue 0432, and
+#    the residual is real: a caller that wrapped this walk in its own
+#    `no_undo 1` is silently disarmed by the restore.
+#  * AN UNPOPPED SUPPRESS SCOPE SILENCES THE USER'S ACTION LOG for the rest of
+#    the session — a worse defect than the navigation noise it removes — so the
+#    pop lives in the same unconditional block as the flags.
+#
+# ============================================================================
+# ⚠ `xschem descend` RETURNS 0 IN TWO CLASSES WITH OPPOSITE RESPONSES (0250)
+# ============================================================================
+# Measured on this tree (actions.c:4055-4065): a refusal BEFORE descending
+# (`no-selection`, `maxdepth`, `not-descendable:<type>`) returns 0 with currsch
+# UNCHANGED, while a blank/missing schematic returns 0 with currsch ALREADY
+# INCREMENTED (`load-failed`). Both PDK prototypes answer 0 with an
+# unconditional `go_back 2` (sg13g2_procs.tcl:401-407): right for the second
+# class, and for the first it pops a level it never pushed — at the top level
+# go_back is a no-op so nobody noticed, but one level down it corrupts sch_path
+# for the rest of the walk and the walk re-visits levels, emitting DUPLICATE
+# cards. Reachable for real at CADMAXHIER=40 on a recursive design. Issue 0433.
+# So the decision is driven off `xschem get currsch` before/after, NEVER off
+# descend's return value. Both prototypes still carry the bug; S5 deletes them.
+#
+# RESIDUAL, unmeasured: a VECTOR DEVICE instance (`name=M1[3:0]` on a symbol
+# that has a descriptor) gets ONE card set built from the literal bracketed
+# name. Both prototypes have the same hole. Issue 0439.
+
+namespace eval op_annot {
+  ## Cards accumulated by the CURRENT walk, and the warnings from the most
+  ## recent one. Namespace state rather than a `global <pdk>_sch_expand` array
+  ## (the prototype's shape) so nothing leaks into the user's global namespace.
+  variable _acc
+  variable warnings
+  if {![info exists _acc]}      { set _acc {} }
+  if {![info exists warnings]}  { set warnings {} }
+}
+
+## op_annot::last_warnings -> the list of human-readable warnings from the most
+## recent save_cards, {} when it was clean.
+##
+## The prototype's answer to an undescendable instance is a bare
+## `puts "Can not descend into $instname"` (sg13g2_procs.tcl:405), which is
+## invisible under --nogui, unassertable from a test, and lost in a GUI session
+## where nobody watches stdout. A collected list can be shown, tested, or
+## ignored by the caller.
+##
+## ⚠ A FILTERED INSTANCE PRODUCES NO WARNING (decision D7). `spice_ignore` is
+## the ordinary way to keep a documentation-only device on a sheet; one line per
+## skipped device would bury the one warning that matters under hundreds on a
+## real PDK design. This channel stays reserved for cells the walk could not
+## enter.
+proc op_annot::last_warnings {} {
+  variable warnings
+  return $warnings
+}
+
+## util.c:72 `strboolcmp`'s TRUTHY spellings, and only those: any all-digit
+## string that is not zero, or `true`/`on`/`yes` case-insensitively.
+##
+## ⚠ `open` and `short` are NOT here. The C matches them with exact-case
+## `strcmp` in a separate branch (actions.c:1055-1059) and `only_toplevel`
+## accepts neither, so folding them in would over-filter one caller to please
+## another. Each caller adds them explicitly.
+proc op_annot::_boolattr {v} {
+  if {$v eq {}} { return 0 }
+  if {[regexp {^[0-9]+$} $v]} {
+    return [expr {[string trimleft $v 0] ne {}}]
+  }
+  switch -- [string tolower $v] {
+    true - on - yes { return 1 }
+  }
+  return 0
+}
+
+## Does instance <i> carry a `spice_ignore` the SPICE netlister honours, in
+## either of its two homes?
+##
+## Both homes, because skip_instance2 (netlist.c:1235) ORs `inst.flags` with
+## `sym.flags`; `getprop instance <n> spice_ignore` reads inst->prop_ptr and
+## `... cell::spice_ignore` reads sym->prop_ptr, with no fallback between them
+## (scheduler.c:5545-5553). `short` counts because skip_instance() is called
+## with skip_short=1 from spice_netlist.c:209.
+##
+## ⚠ NEVER `xschem translate <i> @spice_ignore` — see the 0437 block above.
+proc op_annot::_ignored_attrs {i} {
+  foreach a {spice_ignore cell::spice_ignore} {
+    if {[catch {xschem getprop instance $i $a} v]} { set v {} }
+    if {$v eq {open} || $v eq {short}} { return 1 }
+    if {[::op_annot::_boolattr $v]} { return 1 }
+  }
+  return 0
+}
+
+## Would `xschem netlist` put instance <i> in the deck rooted at <root>?
+## The netlister's own four classes, in the netlister's own spellings.
+proc op_annot::_netlisted {i root} {
+  ## (a) spice_ignore, instance or symbol, true/open/short.
+  if {[::op_annot::_ignored_attrs $i]} { return 0 }
+
+  ## (b) only_toplevel — INSTANCE attribute only (spice_netlist.c:216 reads
+  ## inst->prop_ptr), truthy spellings only, and dropped only when the cell is a
+  ## SUBCIRCUIT of the deck rather than its top. The walk's deck top is its
+  ## ENTRY cell (D2), so the test is "are we below the entry", NOT "is currsch
+  ## greater than 0" — measured: the same cell walked as the deck top keeps its
+  ## only_toplevel device and walked one level down loses it.
+  if {[catch {xschem getprop instance $i only_toplevel} otl]} { set otl {} }
+  if {[::op_annot::_boolattr $otl]} {
+    if {[catch {xschem get sch_path} p]} { set p {} }
+    if {$p ne [::op_annot::_root $root]} { return 0 }
+  }
+
+  ## (c) lvs_ignore, instance or symbol, gated on the USER's global exactly as
+  ## spice_netlist.c:209 passes it into skip_instance(). The walk READS that
+  ## global and never writes it — it is the user's setting, not the walk's (I4
+  ## in spirit).
+  if {[info exists ::lvs_ignore] && [::op_annot::_boolattr $::lvs_ignore]} {
+    foreach a {lvs_ignore cell::lvs_ignore} {
+      if {[catch {xschem getprop instance $i $a} v]} { set v {} }
+      if {$v eq {open} || $v eq {short}} { return 0 }
+      if {[::op_annot::_boolattr $v]} { return 0 }
+    }
+  }
+  return 1
+}
+
+## May the walk DESCEND into instance <i>?  (decision D6)
+##
+## A separate name from _netlisted although it currently answers the same
+## question, because the two failures are different and independently
+## reachable: a filter that suppresses an ignored instance's own cards but still
+## recurses into it emits a full set of cards for devices that are nowhere in
+## the deck — 0437's defect arriving through a different door.
+proc op_annot::_descendable {i root} {
+  return [::op_annot::_netlisted $i $root]
+}
+
+## The cards for ONE instance, or {} when it contributes none.
+##
+## ⚠ THE 0425 CONSUMER CONTRACT (see the header): skip on a blank DEVPATH, never
+## on a blank DESCRIPTOR. A descriptor whose `match` globs do not claim this
+## cell is still fully registered; emitting its params against an empty device
+## name would write `.save [id]` cards, and per spec landmine 9 ngspice answers
+## a bogus card with a fabricated 0.0 column rather than a blank.
+##
+## ⚠ `deck` BASIS, ALWAYS (issue 0436). The root is threaded from save_cards'
+## entry level; nothing here does path arithmetic of its own.
+##
+## ⚠ NO CATCH. devpath returns {} for every DATA condition, so anything that
+## raises in here is a caller bug or a broken PDK three levels down, and I6's
+## whole point is that such a raise reaches save_cards' restore.
+##
+## `derived` and `pinexpr` are not iterated: neither has a vector in any raw
+## (spec §4.3). `derived` is computed from params after they are read, `pinexpr`
+## is an expression over pin voltages.
+proc op_annot::_cards_for {instname root} {
+  set t [::op_annot::type $instname]
+  if {$t eq {}} { return {} }
+  set d [::op_annot::descriptor $t]
+  if {![dict exists $d params]} { return {} }
+  set dev [::op_annot::devpath $instname deck $root]
+  if {$dev eq {}} { return {} }
+  set out {}
+  foreach row [dict get $d params] {
+    lappend out ".save ${dev}\[[lindex $row 1]\]"
+  }
+  return $out
+}
+
+## Did the descend just issued actually land us in a USABLE child?
+## <c0> is `xschem get currsch` from immediately BEFORE the descend.
+##
+## THIS IS THE 0250 CLASS SPLIT (see the header). Return value of `xschem
+## descend` is deliberately not consulted: it is 0 for both classes.
+##   currsch unchanged  -> class 1, nothing was pushed. NO go_back — the
+##                         prototype's unconditional one pops a caller's level.
+##   currsch advanced, descend_error set -> class 2 (`load-failed`): the
+##                         hierarchy ALREADY advanced, so this one DOES owe a
+##                         go_back before the walk can continue.
+##   currsch advanced, descend_error empty -> success.
+proc op_annot::_descended {c0} {
+  if {[catch {xschem get currsch} c1]} { return 0 }
+  if {$c1 <= $c0} { return 0 }
+  if {[catch {xschem get descend_error} derr]} { set derr {} }
+  if {$derr eq {}} { return 1 }
+  catch {xschem go_back 2}
+  return 0
+}
+
+## The recursion. Ported from sg13g2_hier_sch_expand (sg13g2_procs.tcl:366-421).
+## Appends to the namespace accumulator and returns nothing useful; a raise
+## anywhere inside it is caught exactly once, by save_cards.
+##
+## <root> is the walk's ENTRY sch_path, threaded down unchanged: it is the basis
+## every card is rooted at AND the level `only_toplevel` is measured from.
+proc op_annot::_walk {root} {
+  variable _acc
+  variable warnings
+  global nolist_libs
+  set ninstances [xschem get instances]
+  for {set i 0} {$i < $ninstances} {incr i} {
+    set instname [xschem getprop instance $i name]
+    ## ⚠ `getprop instance <n> cell::type`, NOT `getprop symbol <cell> type`.
+    ## The symbol form RAISES for generator cells (measured: 6 of the 14
+    ## instances of sky130_tests/test_generators) and is the literal trigger of
+    ## issue 0431. The index form resolves for a vector instance name too.
+    set type [::op_annot::type $i]
+
+    ## nolist_libs, verbatim from the prototype: libraries the user has excluded
+    ## from hierarchy traversal (empty in every stock workarea).
+    set skip 0
+    if {[info exists nolist_libs] && [llength $nolist_libs]} {
+      if {[catch {abs_sym_path [xschem getprop instance $i cell::name]} abs_symbol]} {
+        set abs_symbol {}
+      }
+      foreach j $nolist_libs {
+        if {[catch {regexp $j $abs_symbol} hit]} { set hit 0 }
+        if {$hit} { set skip 1 ; break }
+      }
+    }
+    if {$skip} { continue }
+
+    ## Emit BEFORE descending: depth-first, parents before children. Order is
+    ## part of this step's golden. Nothing the netlister would drop gets a card
+    ## (issue 0437).
+    if {[::op_annot::_netlisted $i $root]} {
+      foreach c [::op_annot::_cards_for $instname $root] { lappend _acc $c }
+    }
+
+    if {$type ne {subcircuit}} { continue }
+
+    ## D6: an ignored subcircuit INSTANCE takes its whole subtree out of the
+    ## deck, so the walk must not enter it at all.
+    if {![::op_annot::_descendable $i $root]} { continue }
+
+    ## Vector instances: `expandlabel x2[1:0]` -> `x2[1],x2[0] 2` (never raises,
+    ## answers `x1 1` for a scalar). Descend ONCE, then walk the members with
+    ## change_sch_path, then go_back ONCE.
+    set ninst [lindex [split [xschem expandlabel $instname] { }] 1]
+    for {set n 1} {$n <= $ninst} {incr n} {
+      if {$n == 1} {
+        set c0 [xschem get currsch]
+        ## ⚠ `xschem select instance <i>` TOGGLES, and go_back leaves the
+        ## instance we came from selected — so unselect first or the next
+        ## descend refuses with `no-selection` / picks the wrong target.
+        ## `fast nodraw` is src/xschem.tcl:3717's cheaper form: no status
+        ## traffic, no selection draw.
+        catch {xschem unselect_all}
+        catch {xschem select instance $i fast nodraw}
+        xschem descend $n 2
+        if {[catch {xschem get descend_error} derr]} { set derr {} }
+        if {![::op_annot::_descended $c0]} {
+          lappend warnings "cannot descend into $instname ($derr)"
+          break
+        }
+      }
+      if {$n > 1} { xschem change_sch_path $n }
+      ::op_annot::_walk $root
+      if {$n == $ninst} { xschem go_back 2 }
+    }
+  }
+  return 1
+}
+
+## Ascend back to the level the walk started from — and NO FURTHER.
+##
+## Bounded by the ENTRY currsch, not by 0 (see I6 in the header). The guards are
+## because this runs on an unattended error path: a go_back that refuses to move
+## must break the loop rather than spin it forever.
+proc op_annot::_unwind {entry} {
+  set target [lindex $entry 0]
+  set guard 0
+  while {1} {
+    if {[catch {xschem get currsch} c]} { break }
+    if {$c <= $target} { break }
+    if {[catch {xschem go_back 2}]} { break }
+    if {[catch {xschem get currsch} c2]} { break }
+    if {$c2 >= $c} { break }
+    if {[incr guard] > 64} { break }
+  }
+}
+
+## THE UNCONDITIONAL RESTORE (I6). Every line is individually catch-wrapped so
+## one failure cannot skip the rest — the prototypes' straight-line reset is
+## simply not reached when the walk raises, which is issue 0431.
+##
+## <rc> is the catch code of the walk. It is deliberately NOT branched on: the
+## whole point is that this block runs identically on both paths.
+proc op_annot::_restore {entry rc} {
+  catch {::op_annot::_unwind $entry}
+  ## no_undo has no getter (issue 0432), so 0 is the only restorable value.
+  catch {xschem set no_undo 0}
+  catch {xschem set no_draw [lindex $entry 2]}
+  catch {set ::keep_symbols [lindex $entry 3]}
+  catch {xschem log_action -suppress pop}
+}
+
+## `.save all` first (I2 / rule R2, DOT-card — see the header), then the cards.
+## No prose header: write_save_file adds the human one, and S4's deck consumer
+## would only have to strip it.
+proc op_annot::_block {cards} {
+  if {[llength $cards] == 0} { return {} }
+  return "[join [linsert $cards 0 {.save all}] \n]\n"
+}
+
+## op_annot::save_cards -> the `.save` block for the whole hierarchy below the
+## CURRENT cell, as text; {} when no device produced a card.
+##
+## The block is rooted HERE: every card is `deck`-based with this level's
+## `sch_path` as its root, so the file is a save block for a deck of the cell
+## you are standing in, and no loaded raw can move a single name (issue 0436).
+##
+## ⚠ IT RE-RAISES. A partial block silently returned is spec landmine 9's
+## fabricated-zeros hazard wearing a different hat: the devices the walk never
+## reached do not blank in the raw, they get a 0.0 column. Callers (including
+## S4's render_deck) must call this inside a catch.
+##
+## I4: reads context, never writes it. No instance placed, no set_modify,
+## nothing written to the .sch.
+proc op_annot::save_cards {} {
+  variable _acc
+  variable warnings
+  set _acc {}
+  set warnings {}
+  set kd 0
+  if {[info exists ::keep_symbols]} { set kd $::keep_symbols }
+  set entry [list [xschem get currsch] [xschem get sch_path] \
+                  [xschem get no_draw] $kd]
+  ## THE ROOT OF THIS BLOCK. Read once, before anything moves.
+  set root [lindex $entry 1]
+  ## Pushed OUTSIDE the catch and popped inside _restore, so the pair survives
+  ## a raise. descend and go_back self-log (actions.c:4073, :4229) and a walk
+  ## over a real design would otherwise flood a log whose contract is
+  ## REPLAYABLE USER EDITS with hundreds of navigation lines.
+  catch {xschem log_action -suppress push}
+  set rc [catch {
+    set ::keep_symbols 1
+    ## The prototype's entry unselect_all, kept: descend acts on the selection,
+    ## so this is a real precondition. The selection is NOT saved and restored —
+    ## `xschem selected_set` answers instance NAMES only, so a "restore" would
+    ## silently drop every wire and text the user had selected, which is more
+    ## surprising than clearing it.
+    xschem unselect_all
+    xschem set no_draw 1
+    xschem set no_undo 1
+    ::op_annot::_walk $root
+  } res opts]
+  ::op_annot::_restore $entry $rc
+  if {$rc} { return -options $opts $res }
+  return [::op_annot::_block $_acc]
+}
+
+## op_annot::write_save_file -> writes the block to
+## $netlist_dir/<current cell>.save and returns the path; {} when there was
+## nothing to save (and then NO file is written — a .save file containing only
+## a header says nothing while reporting success).
+##
+## ⚠ THE FILENAME AGREES WITH THE BODY, and only because the cards are
+## entry-relative (D2): `<current cell>.save` describes a deck whose top IS the
+## current cell, which is also the cell `xschem netlist` would write from here.
+##
+## Modelled on IHP's `Create FET and BIP .save file` menu command
+## (sg13g2_procs.tcl:602-606), including its `file mkdir $netlist_dir`, which
+## sky130_procs.tcl:235 lacks. The textwindow is X-only: under --nogui / --pipe
+## there is no Tk and `textwindow` would raise.
+##
+## Definition only at source time — the SOURCE-TIME RULE above. Nothing here
+## runs until a menu item or a script calls it.
+proc op_annot::write_save_file {} {
+  global netlist_dir
+  set block [::op_annot::save_cards]
+  if {$block eq {}} { return {} }
+  file mkdir $netlist_dir
+  set path [file join $netlist_dir \
+    "[file rootname [file tail [xschem get current_name]]].save"]
+  ## ASCII only: this line goes into a file a SPICE parser reads.
+  write_data "* operating-point .save cards - .include this file in your testbench\n\n$block" $path
+  if {[info exists ::has_x] && $::has_x} { catch {textwindow $path} }
+  return $path
+}
